{
  "image_name": "0de6795d7b4ce4e73c3389e89f678cab.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how tumour-associated macrophages (TAMs) interact with cancer cells and how those interactions can be therapeutically redirected. The central idea is that tumour cells display several “don’t eat me” and immunosuppressive signals that restrain macrophage function. In the left panel, inhibitory receptor–ligand pairs such as SIRPα–CD47, LILRB1–HLA1, SIGLEC10–CD24, and PD1–PDL1 are shown bridging the TAM and tumour cell, leading to inhibition of phagocytosis and reduced activation. The TAM is also depicted with markers associated with suppressive macrophage states, suggesting an immunosuppressive phenotype within the tumour environment.\n\nThe right side shows the desired effects of blocking these inhibitory pathways, especially CD47–SIRPα, often together with tumour-targeting antibodies. Once the brake on macrophages is removed, antibody-coated tumour cells are more effectively recognized through Fc receptors, which enhances antibody-dependent phagocytosis or cytotoxicity. Macrophage uptake of tumour material also appears to improve antigen presentation through MHC II to T cells, linking innate and adaptive immunity.\n\nA final schematic highlights a safety strategy using bispecific antibodies, designed to preserve tumour killing while avoiding unwanted binding to normal CD47-expressing cells such as red blood cells and platelets.",
